---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConfirmOfficial from "../components/ConfirmOfficial.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Three things people confuse"
  description="Andorran immigration, tax residency and buying a house are three different tests. Mixing them is expensive."
  path="/residency-vs-tax"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Three things people confuse</h1>
    <p>
      The permit lets you stay. Tax residency decides who taxes you. The
      house is only an asset — unless the permit requires it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <ConfirmOfficial />

    <p>
      If you take one thing from this site, take this. Andorra runs
      three systems that share a vocabulary and do not share rules.
      People who collapse them into “moving to Andorra” are the people
      who later discover they still have a tax home in Spain.
    </p>

    <h2>1. The immigration permit</h2>
    <p>
      This is the card Immigration issues. It answers: may this person
      live in Andorra, and may they work. The main private routes in
      2026 are residence without work (passive / no lucrative activity)
      and residence with work, including self-employment through an
      Andorran company. There are also employee permits, family reunion,
      and a digital-nomad style route. Each has its own days, deposits
      and prohibitions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Passive presence is generally 90 days in the calendar year.
      Self-employed active presence is generally 183 days. Those numbers
      belong to immigration. They are not a tax opinion.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Tax residency</h2>
    <p>
      Andorran personal income tax (IRPF) treats you as tax resident if
      you spend more than 183 days in Andorra in the calendar year,
      counting ordinary absences unless you prove tax residence
      elsewhere, or if your main centre of economic interests is here. A
      spouse and minor children habitually living here can also create a
      presumption. That is Llei 5/2014, article 8, not the immigration
      leaflet.
    </p>
    <p>
      So a passive permit that only requires 90 days does not make you
      an Andorran tax resident by itself. If you still live most of the
      year in Spain or France, keep the house, the school run and the
      business there, those countries can still claim you. Andorra has
      information-exchange and double-tax arrangements. It is not a
      place you disappear to.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Government’s own FAQ is
      <a href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/l/4191384" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >who is a tax resident</a
      >.
      A longer reading of that article is on
      <a href="/tax-residence">tax residence</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>3. The house</h2>
    <p>
      Buying property does not grant a permit. A permit does not force
      you to buy. Passive residency in 2026 often uses property as part
      of the one-million-euro investment, with a floor of €800,000 per
      dwelling, but you can also use other qualifying Andorran assets.
      Non-residents and residents with under three years’ effective
      residence pay an extra foreign-investment tax on purchases.
      Residents of longer standing generally do not. That tax is a
      purchase cost. It is not a residency application.
    </p>

    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Immigration permit</th>
          <th>Tax residency</th>
          <th>Buying a house</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Question</td>
          <td>May I live / work here?</td>
          <td>Who taxes my worldwide income?</td>
          <td>May I acquire this building, and at what tax?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Typical test</td>
          <td>90 or 183 days, investment, AFA sum, clean record</td>
          <td>183 days or centre of interests</td>
          <td>Foreign-investment authorisation + ITP ± IIEI</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Authority</td>
          <td>Immigration / AFA</td>
          <td>Tax administration (IRPF)</td>
          <td>Notary, Tributs, Economy</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Common error</td>
          <td>Assuming the card equals 10% tax</td>
          <td>Assuming 90 days is enough</td>
          <td>Assuming the deed is the permit</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What the tax actually is, if you are resident</h2>
    <p>
      IRPF is 0% on the first €24,000, 5% on the next band to €40,000,
      and 10% above that. There is no wealth tax and, in the ordinary
      case, no inheritance tax. Companies pay a 10% headline corporate
      tax. IGI, the VAT equivalent, is 4.5%. Those figures are why
      people come. They are not a reason to ignore the 183-day test or a
      Spanish Hacienda challenge on “centre of vital interests”.
    </p>

    <h2>Which route is which</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        You will live here most of the year and work or run a company
        here — active / self-employed. See
        <a href="/company-active-residency">Company and active residency</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can live on assets or foreign income and will not work in
        Andorra — passive. See
        <a href="/passive-residency">Passive residency in 2026</a>.
        Budget a million in Andorran assets plus the AFA payment, and
        still plan the tax-residence question separately.
      </li>
      <li>
        You want the house as a second home and will not live here —
        that is a property purchase, not a life plan. See
        <a href="/buying-property">Buying property</a>.
        You will pay the foreign-investment tax.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Andorra is not in the EU and not in Schengen in the ordinary
      sense, though border arrangements with the EU have been
      tightening. Do not sell yourself a “EU golden visa”. The local
      name is residència sense activitat lucrativa, or residència
      passiva. People search “Andorra golden visa” because other
      countries trained them to. The phrase is a useful search term and
      a bad legal description.
    </p>
    <p>
      No page on this site should say a 90-day permit equals tax
      residency. Read
      <a href="/tax-residence">tax residence</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
